  "brief_description": "This is the third in a series of four panels illustrating the different times of day that Corot painted for his friend, fellow artist Alexandre-Gabriel Decamps. It is evening and colours are at their richest. Nearest to us, a mysterious figure is wearing what appears to be a dark brown monk’s habit with a hood, possibly Capuchin robes. Two women are in a boat on the shoreline. One, in a white and pink dress, is sitting, while the other, in a white dress and yellow shawl, stands as she holds an instrument that may be a mandolin.\n\nThe women’s clothing, landscape setting and the presence of music suggest a fête galante, a type of picture showing elegantly attired men and women in a parkland setting which was particularly popular at the court of Versailles in the eighteenth century. The lakeside location ringed by tall sinuous trees with billowing feathery foliage also has echoes of French Rococo painting.",
